So I'm going to turn this over to Evan.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:24.570
Thank you Peter.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.570
Hello everybody.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.570
It is nice to see we have a nice audience
out there.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:35.570
I am going to talk to you about PubChem, as
a source of laboratory chemical safety information.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:41.150
If you are not aware what PubChem is as a
resource, I will give you a quick overview.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.780
We're centered here at the National Center
for Biotechnology Information.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:52.780
We contain the chemical content that is found
within the NCBI resource, called PubChem.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:59.160
This is our home page, I encourage you to
explore it, especially the Services tab the

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.470
top.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:05.509
That way you can have a greater familiarity
with the resource.

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:12.660
Our overall goal is to be open archive of
public information.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:20.580
Our key mission has evolved slightly as a
function of time, what we aim to do is provide

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.380
biological role and function of chemical substances.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:28.610
And health and safety is within that scope.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.060
PubChem is a resource, content changes on
a daily basis.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:39.600
If data is not there one day, you can check
back and it may be there the next.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.900
The hope for this webinar is you will be able
to understand three primary things.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.830
PubChem Compound is a source of information
for laboratory chemical safety data.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:57.220
And the hope here is that you can understand
the LCSS, PubChem's Labortory Chemical Safety

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:58.380
Summary.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:04.709
The source and content where you can access
the information.

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:13.480
But also how you can use and access it relative
to laboratory safety practices.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:22.050
There is quite a number of reasons for this
webinar to be important for many people.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:27.440
For one, there are three hazmat responses
to laboratories every week, academic research

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:33.190
laboratories account for about half of all
hazmat responses in the US.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.600
And teaching labs account for 10%.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:42.040
Education is the third highest hazmat response
sector.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.160
Since 2010, the chemical safety board has
produced two major reports, on chemical safety

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.990
incidents in research and teaching laboratory
settings.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:56.510
These are based on a classroom methanol fire
in 2006, and a serioes of research lab eventes

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.320
from 2008 to 2011.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:05.840
There has been an ongoing series of laboratory
hazmat events since then.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:13.040
It's accumulated a history of chemical accidents,
which have called into question the traditional

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.840
lab safety paradigm of don't make mistakes
and you won't have accidents.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.470
And this led to more concern about the safety
culture of laboratories.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:28.459
This has been addressed in reports by the
ACS and National Research Council.

00:03:28.459 --> 00:03:34.360
Chemical health and safety data sources are
really blessed in many ways by having so many

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.319
data sources available.

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:42.040
This however presents an issue, in that there
can be gaps or errors in the information.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:49.040
If all the information was available, will
decisions made by chemists change.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.620
Every scientist you can think of, they may
not be aware of the various sources of information.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:04:00.590
I have presented here a fraction of those
available.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:05.140
Couple into this the fact that laboratory
chemical safety information is evolving.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.510
There has been a recent transition from Material
Data Safety Sheets, the so-called MSDS, to

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:16.820
the global marmonization system, GHS, safety
data sheets, otherwise known as SDS.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:22.150
This was a major step forward in part because
it provided a standardization of the information

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:27.190
content that would be available.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:35.910
This has been further evolving through the
National research Council, put into practices

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.190
in the laboratory handling and management
of chemical hazards publication.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:45.280
This was a step forward for laboratory chemical
users and described issues of scale related

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.180
to laboratory use of chemicals.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:58.040
It provided a set of properties and a format
to be used.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:07.170
The PubChem LCSS mirrored that content as
a subset of information that is available

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.940
within PubChem as a whole.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:16.270
It includes various types of Hazzard safety
information such as flammability, toxicity,

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:19.540
exposure limits, symptoms and cleanup.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:23.071
This evolution is important technically as
well as culturally, because chemists have

00:05:23.071 --> 00:05:29.410
traditionally been disappointed by the limited
value of the MSDS.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:36.360
You can imagine that with LCSS, this enables
all available health and safety information

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.680
or a subset of this and making it available
for ready use.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:48.169
One of the reasons this is important relative
to PubChem is because it provides a wide breadth

00:05:48.169 --> 00:05:53.930
of information and helps to move chemical
safety information resources from the limitations

00:05:53.930 --> 00:06:01.010
of the 20th century paper media to leverage
21st-century chemical information technology.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:07.690
We go one step further than the traditional
SDS that you might otherwise get via GHS,

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:15.850
by including other information such asspecific
reactivity information with other reagents.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:23.610
So if you look at a typical property, such
as Boiling point, you can see who is providing

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:27.850
what information from various authoritative
resources.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:32.350
A common hazmat rule is that three resources
have to agree on a property before an operational

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.970
decision should be made based on that property.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:40.139
PubChem's collection of safety information
allows for a direct comparison of the authoritative

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:44.190
data sources.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:51.220
You can access LCSS and PubChem from any page
that has this content, you will see a safety

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:57.710
summary link inside the page, that will give
you a laboratory chemical safety summary data

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:00.250
view for that same record.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:10.199
But the content is reformatted in such a way
that is more in line in the GHS/SDS sheets,

00:07:10.199 --> 00:07:15.980
but also to make for easy comparisons.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.120
There is a fair bit of chemical safety data
content inside PubChem.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:25.480
What you are looking at here is a data view
from the PubChem Classification browser, where

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:31.610
we're showing a classification tree for our
PubChem Compound table of contents.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:39.170
Basically for the various information pieces
inside PubChem, you can see we have over 100,000

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:43.570
records with safety and hazard information
content, the numbers you see are the number

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:49.130
of chemical compound records inside PubCehem
that contain certain types of property or

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:51.930
property types.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:57.860
For instance, for GHS classifications, there
are about 102,000 chemicals that have this

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.240
type of information content.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:06.180
In addition you can see in contrast the stability
and reactivity information content is limited

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.840
to 5700 records.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:15.680
Most coming through the national library of
medicine's hazardous substances databank HSDB

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.190
system.

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:24.680
In terms of global harmonization, you might
find that different data sources provide different

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:27.550
degrees of GHS classificaiton.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:36.079
The nice thing here is that PubChem has provided
a subset of the GHS contents available.

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:41.369
Important to this, is the information content
that comes from the European chemical agency

00:08:41.369 --> 00:08:45.869
through the reach efforts that they have in
Europe.

00:08:45.869 --> 00:08:50.399
One thing you will notice is that PubChem
records will aggregate content.

00:08:50.399 --> 00:08:55.769
So, if you see here for acetone, there are
78 different notifications provided by over

00:08:55.769 --> 00:08:58.709
3500 different companies.

00:08:58.709 --> 00:09:04.390
But you can see when you compare this information
content between the different sources, some

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:12.240
data sources such as that from the Japanese
NITE data source, this includes additional

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:17.639
GHS Hazzard codes that you will not find for
other records.

00:09:17.639 --> 00:09:21.910
In part, being able to compare these different
data sources and then going back to the original

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:27.149
data source to find out why perhaps they have
selected these different Hazzard codes, you

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:33.339
can be provided more informed information
in terms of decisions.

00:09:33.339 --> 00:09:40.369
However, there are some issues with the information
content that you can get through PubChem in

00:09:40.369 --> 00:09:47.029
part because a chemical structure record could
have multiple forms of an entity associated

00:09:47.029 --> 00:09:48.029
with it.

00:09:48.029 --> 00:09:54.920
As we see here in the case of formaldehyde,
there are over 4000 companies providing different

00:09:54.920 --> 00:10:04.689
notifications, and we aggregated this information
content, to let you know what percentage of

00:10:04.689 --> 00:10:11.439
these notifications that are coming from these
company's are reporting various types of Hazzard

00:10:11.439 --> 00:10:12.610
codes.

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:18.649
They may correspond to different forms of
the entity, that is of importance to your

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:21.709
use case or may not be.

00:10:21.709 --> 00:10:27.560
You will have to examine and see what are
the different records and what types of information

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.800
content is available.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:38.170
A main here is that PubChem opens up new options
for using safety data in a practical way,

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:42.540
by giving safety data a very customized form
via data streams.

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:52.209
You can download the entire content, go to
the PubChem FTP site, and download a zipped

00:10:52.209 --> 00:11:00.970
compressed XML file, that has the entire content
of what PubChem knows relative to LCSS.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:04.860
We update this on a daily basis.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:12.579
We also provide extensive provenence information,
where you can explore data sources, and for

00:11:12.579 --> 00:11:15.600
instance limit them to annotation sources.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:22.379
So out of the 522 sources, there are 57 that
provide annotation content, you can examine

00:11:22.379 --> 00:11:29.110
each individual record, for instance, for
the European chemicals agency, you can see

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:34.740
they provide us with various types of information
content such as GHS classification information,

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:41.740
and you can download that content in a datastream
that may be helpful and informative for your

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:42.819
purposes.

00:11:42.819 --> 00:11:46.679
You can also download the information content
on-the-fly.

00:11:46.679 --> 00:11:52.730
The earlier PubChem table of contents, also
lists the information sources, so that you

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:58.839
can see how many chemical records have information
from the data source.

00:11:58.839 --> 00:12:04.470
In summary the PubChem laboratory chemical
safety summary contains basic information,

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:13.160
raw data, GHS ossification and more that are
important for you to make informed choices.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:17.860
Or to locate information sources, to make
informed choices.

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:21.399
It's also an important piece of the ongoing
evolution of the laboratory chemical safety

00:12:21.399 --> 00:12:28.459
information that helps you move away from
paper, and more towards simple way for you

00:12:28.459 --> 00:12:35.420
as a human to read that content, compare the
content, and to work with it in a computer

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.379
readable way.

00:12:37.379 --> 00:12:39.660
In part because it's downloadable in bulk.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:45.819
And we have a breadth of data, over 100,000
chemicals, but also depth in the number of

00:12:45.819 --> 00:12:51.970
entities which have other properties and information
content that is important.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:57.329
It provides an extensive provenence trial
to allow you to understand which data sources

00:12:57.329 --> 00:13:01.449
that are authoritative, and how we do the
data linking.

